WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.040
- Another banger from the
Twitter account Bad Legal Takes.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.220
Someone from ABC was
asking to use a video clip

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.410
that was taken on a plane.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.357
To which another person
helpfully responds,

00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:12.780
"Them asking was a formality.
Once you post something

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.580
on the internet, it's out
there for the world to see

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.930
and use as they please,
without having to pay

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.300
or ask for your
authorization to use legally,

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.650
they just have to credit the person

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.580
who recorded and posted."

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:23.470
No! Agh!

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.210
Look, this is a common
misconception about copyright law,

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.050
but it is very, very wrong.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.630
When things enter the public domain,

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.260
yes, you can use them for any purpose.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.130
But for something to
enter the public domain,

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:38.050
they basically need to have
their copyright term expired.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.540
That generally means they have
to be 100 or 120 years old.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.960
Just because something
is public doesn't mean

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.050
it's in the public domain.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.440
Just because something is on the internet,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.700
doesn't mean you can use it
for whatever reason you want.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:50.700
Don't do that.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.820
If you use stuff that's on the internet

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.553
without getting the proper
rights, I will see you in court.

